A||||14||pHoOl||Tire shining solution||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||08-06-2000||01:08 PM||||Anybody have any good recommendations for things to make those tires look like they are mirrors?  I've tried regular Armor-All, and now i'm trying some stuff called Tire Wet.  Just thought i'd let people post with what works best, since i dont' have the cash to be expirimenting at five bucks a pop for different bottles of stuff.  Thanks all!||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||08-06-2000||02:14 PM||||I have tried them all...Tire Wet wins hands down. Best way to apply it is to get an old car wax applicator (clean, of course), spray this with the Tire Wet, and then apply it to your tires. This keeps it off your wheels, which is important, because it does seem to attract dust. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=64144&a=464935&p=25646990"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||stimpy||08-06-2000||02:33 PM||||Used motor oil!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||pHoOl||08-06-2000||04:45 PM||||yeah, used motor oil would work, especially with the added traction it would give you =)||||reg||12
Z||000004||olympic||08-06-2000||08:00 PM||||That new stuff from Castrol is suppose to be very good.  I forget what the name of it it though.<P>------------------<BR>"Those who can,  do.  Those who can't,  teach."||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||08-06-2000||08:39 PM||||I'll admit that shiny tires look good but with all the mods I have done with my suspension I would never use any of the products since they hamper the handling if the car. Something about putting a slick coating on my tires just doesn't work for me.<BR>I prefer functionality over looks. Of course this is just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||08-06-2000||10:50 PM||||does it affect handling much if you just put it on the outside, only the part that people see, not the part that touches the ground?  speaking of handling, i was going about 30 around a right turn in a subdivision today and the front end was screamin'.  what kinds of things and how much weight should i put down in the front to compensate?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||08-06-2000||10:57 PM||||Depending on how "thin" the stuff you put on is, Any excess would go to the outside edge of the tire as you drive. If you go into a hard turn with 60 or 70 series tires, odds are the sidewall will roll slightly which is where the stuff is. If you read the back of the bottle on any of them they caution that it can affect steering and handling of the car. I have a tendency to take turns rather hard. Especially highway exit ramps. So these products would cause me problems. I will admit that for show a car looks great with the tires shined up.<P>As for your screaming tires, What size are they? How old are they? I have 225/50R15's and I can take a regular corner at about 40 without a squeel from the tires. I have the car lowered and stiffened too so this aids my handling. If you have 70 series tires try going to a 60 series. You could even get a higher performance orientated tire too. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||pHoOl||08-07-2000||12:56 AM||||they are the stock size 205x60 i think is what they are.  don't know age, but haven't changed since we got it in february.  maybe i'll leave the shiner for the rear or use a rag to wipe excess.  any thoughts on the weight idea?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolemblem.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||08-07-2000||01:27 AM||||I use Turtle Wax "Tire Wax" and make sire I wipe it down good before I drive away. I also am careful not to go to the very edge of the tire when putting it on. Like Oreif says, the bottle cautions you about handling -- another thing to think about when using the waxes is that the centrifugal force of the tires during rotation will cause any liquid or paste not wiped down good to flow to the outer edge of the tires......I haven't experienced any handling problems, even at the on ramps (course i keep ALL my wheels on the ground when I go around them - Oreif, are you doing that two wheel thang??)......||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||08-07-2000||01:55 AM||||No I don't 2-wheel the ramps, all 4 wheels are on the ground and I have no tire roll, I generally slow down to about 85mph.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||08-07-2000||02:04 AM||||Back when I was showing my trucks, I just used the liquid wax.  Nothing special, just the same stuff I used on the body.  Works great and you don't have the same petroleum-based-slicker-than-wet-owl-crap problem.  Wax on, wax off.  Just stop at the edge of the tire before you get to the tread.  Only problem is that it gets dirty very quickly.  I wouldn't recommend this for everyday use, just for shows or cruising night.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Master_Sushi||08-07-2000||10:29 AM||||I have heard if you get armor all on your brake rotors it can warp them. I dont know if this it true or not, but supposedly if it gets on one side of your rotor, it gets the other side less hot then the other. Causing them to warp?<P>I was told this by a guy working at a brake shop. I dont know how true it is or anything. But I was just wondering if anyone else has heard anything about it.<P>Thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 08-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||tgowens||08-07-2000||01:15 PM||||Oreif, I always thought those ramp signs said <I>35 mph</I> -- I need new glasses - never realized that was <I>85</I>!!! thanks!!||||reg||1
Z||000014||HugGRRRFiero||08-07-2000||01:49 PM||||  I like to put just enough of that greasy stuff on my tires to get them nice and black, but not so much that they look like they've got a good high gloss clear gelcoat finish.  Anyone see that commercial of the guy using the tires off his Chevelle as a reflector for a good tan?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> hehe.  Just my preference...<P>  I've found Armor-all & son-of-a-gun works good.  Tire wet works very good for the high gloss look.  I don't need to use much anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif"> <BR> <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||6
